Fang2024.pdf.md:24:Keywords: Psychiatric disorders Transdiagnostic subtypes Normative modeling Epicenter
Fang2024.pdf.md:42:Psychiatric disorders exhibit a shared neuropathology, yet the diverse presentations among patients necessitate the identification of transdiagnostic subtypes to enhance diagnostic and treatment strategies. This study aims to unveil  potential  transdiagnostic  subtypes  based  on  personalized  gray  matter  morphological  abnormalities.  A total of 496 patients with psychiatric disorders and 255 healthy controls (HCs) from three distinct datasets (one for discovery and two for validation) were enrolled. Individualized gray matter morphological abnormalities were determined using normative modeling to identify transdiagnostic subtypes. In the discovery dataset, two transdiagnostic subtypes with contrasting patterns of structural abnormalities compared to HCs were identified. Reproducibility and generalizability analyses  demonstrated that these  subtypes could be generalized to  new patients and even to new disorders in the validation datasets. These subtypes were characterized by distinct disease epicenters. The gray matter abnormal pattern in subtype 1 was mainly linked to excitatory receptors, whereas subtype 2 showed a predominant association with inhibitory receptors. Furthermore, we observed that the gray matter abnormal pattern in subtype 2 was correlated with transcriptional profiles of inflammationrelated genes, while subtype 1 did not show this association. Our findings reveal two robust transdiagnostic biotypes, offering novel insights into psychiatric nosology.
Fang2024.pdf.md:62:regions (Chang et al., 2018). However, psychiatric disorders are highly heterogeneous syndromes and this variability among cases results in inconsistent or conflicting findings (Chen et al., 2016). Even within a single diagnosis, neuroimaging studies have reported reduced, increased, or even no differential gray matter volumes (L ´ azaro et al., 2011; Okasha et al., 2000). These results suggest the existence of distinct neuroanatomical subtypes in psychiatric disorders. Indeed, studies have identified subtypes with opposing patterns of structural abnormalities within a single diagnosis, such  as schizophrenia and  obsessivecompulsive  disorder  (Chand  et  al.,  2020;  Han  et  al.,  2022b).  To address this heterogeneity, normative modeling has been proposed to characterize structural abnormalities at the individual level by assessing deviations from the normal distribution (Marquand et al., 2016). Using these  individualized  structural  deviations  as  features,  a  recent  study successfully identified two depression biotypes with distinct patterns of cortical thinning and treatment responses (Li et al., 2024). However, whether individualized structural deviations could reveal transdiagnostic subtypes remains unknown.
Fang2024.pdf.md:66:In this study, we aimed to identify distinct transdiagnostic subtypes based  on  individualized  gray  matter  morphological  abnormalities compared to normal expectations. We included a total of 496 patients with psychiatric disorders and 255 healthy controls from three independent datasets (one discovery dataset and two validation datasets). First,  we  derived  individualized  deviation  maps  (individualized  gray matter  morphological  abnormalities)  using  normative  modeling  and used  them  to  determine  transdiagnostic  subtypes  in  the  discovery dataset. We then conducted a series of sensitivity analyses to examine the  reproducibility  and  generalizability  of  the  identified  subtypes  in unseen patients and even in unseen disorders in the validation datasets. Subsequently, we investigated the disease epicenters of the identified subtypes. To gain deeper insights into the molecular and genetic underpinnings of the subtypes, we further investigated the associations between structural abnormalities in these subtypes and the distribution of neurotransmitter receptors/transporters, as well as the transcriptional profiles of inflammation-related genes. The workflow of this study is shown in Fig. 1.
Fang2024.pdf.md:74:Fig. 1. The workflow of this study. For each brain region, individualized gray matter volume/morphological (GMV) abnormalities are obtained using normative modeling and used to identify transdiagnostic subtypes. A series of sensitivity analyses are conducted to assess the reproducibility and generalizability of clustering outcomes. To gain deeper insights into the subtypes, we investigate their disease epicenters, and molecular and genetic underpinnings.
Fang2024.pdf.md:84:First,  we  estimated  individualized  gray  matter  morphological  abnormalities using the normative modeling. Following methods used in previous  studies  (Han  et  al.,  2023a;  Marquand  et  al.,  2016;  Wolfers et al., 2018), we trained a Gaussian process regression model to estimate the normative range of regional gray matter volumes (GMVs) based on age and sex in HCs. This trained model was then applied to each patient, generating predicted GMVs. Subsequently, Z scores (representing individualized abnormalities) were calculated to quantify deviations of the predicted GMVs from normal distributions. Positive Z scores indicated higher GMVs in patients compared to HCs, and vice versa. This process resulted in a Z -score vector (246 × 1) for each patient, representing their individualized gray matter morphological abnormalities.
Fang2024.pdf.md:112:Then, we investigated the association between neurotransmitter receptors/transporters profiles and gray matter gray matter morphological abnormalities of the identified subtypes.  Neurotransmitter receptors/ transporters  were  obtained  from  PET-derived  neurotransmitter  receptors/transporters atlas collected and shared by Hansen and Shafiei (2022b). The neurotransmitter receptors/transporters include serotonin (5HT1A  (Savli et al., 2012), 5HT1B  (Baldassarri et al., 2018; Gallezot et al., 2010; Matuskey et al., 2014; Murrough et al., 2011a; Murrough et al., 2011b; Saricicek et al., 2015; Savli et al., 2012), 5HT2A (Beliveau and Ganz, 2017), 5HT4 (Beliveau and Ganz, 2017), 5HT6 (Radhakrishnan  et  al.,  2020;  Radhakrishnan  et  al.,  2018),  5HTT (Beliveau and Ganz, 2017)), norepinephrine ( α 4 β 2  (Baldassarri et al., 2018;  Hillmer  et  al.,  2016),  M1 (Naganawa  et  al.,  2021),  VAChT (Aghourian  et  al.,  2017;  Bedard  et  al.,  2019)),  cannabinoid  (CB1 (D ' Souza et al., 2016; Neumeister et al., 2012; Normandin et al., 2015; Ranganathan  et  al.,  2016)),  dopamine  (D1 (Kaller  et  al.,  2017),  D2 (Sandiego et al., 2015; Slifstein et al., 2015; Smith et al., 2019; Zakiniaeiz et al., 2019), DAT (Dukart et al., 2018)), GABA (GABAa (N ø rgaard et al., 2021)), histamine (H3 (Gallezot et al., 2017)), glutamate (mGluR5 (DuBois et al., 2016; Smart et al., 2019), NMDA (Galovic et al., 2021; McGinnity et al., 2014)), opioid (MOR (Kantonen et al., 2020)), and norepinephrine (NET (Belfort-DeAguiar et al., 2018; Ding et al., 2010; Li et al., 2014; Sanchez-Rangel et al., 2020)). PET images were averaged across participants within each study, normalized to the MNI-ICBM 152 non-linear 2009 template, and then parcellated into 246 brain regions. The  average  regional  neurotransmitter  receptor/transporter  densities were then Z -scored (Hansen and Shafiei, 2022a, 2022b). For each subtype,  we  constructed  a  multilinear  model  between  neurotransmitter receptors/transporters profiles and gray matter differential pattern. The significance  of  these  multilinear  models  was  evaluated  through  permutation testing (10,000 times), false discovery rate (FDR) correction.
Fang2024.pdf.md:114:Subsequently, to better understand the relative importance of each predictor (in this case, neurotransmitter receptors/transporters profiles) in contributing to the model ' s overall predictive power, we conducted a dominance  analysis  for  each  multilinear  model.  Dominance  analysis helps determine how each predictor impacts the model ' s fit by considering all possible predictor combinations within the model (Budescu and David, 1993). This is achieved by applying the multilinear model to every potential subset of predictors, resulting in 2 n -1 subset models for a model with n predictors. In our study, we utilized the concept of total dominance to gauge each predictor ' s relative significance (Budescu and David, 1993; Hansen and Shafiei, 2022a). Total dominance is calculated as  the  mean  increase  in  explained  variance  (R 2 )  that  each  predictor contributes across all subset models in which it is included (Azen and Budescu, 2003; Budescu and David, 1993).
Fang2024.pdf.md:162:Next,  we  delved  into  exploring  the  connections  between  neurotransmitter receptors/transporters and gray matter morphological abnormalities  within  transdiagnostic  subtypes.  This  was  achieved  by constructing two multilinear models that accounted for the spatial distributions of receptors/transporters and the differential patterns of gray matter morphological abnormalities in each subtype. The goodness-offit  of  these  models  (adjusted  R 2 )  was  0.16  (F-statistic  (246,226) = 3.41) for subtype 1 and 0.59 (F-statistic (246,226) = 19.70) for subtype 2, indicating strong model performance (see Fig. 5A). Notably, all FDRcorrected permutation p -values were &lt; 0.001. Furthermore, our analysis utilizing  dominance analysis revealed key neurotransmitter receptors associated with each subtype. Specifically, 5HT1A emerged as significant for subtype 1, whereas mGluR5 was identified as crucial for subtype 2 (see Fig. 5B).
Fang2024.pdf.md:188:Fig. 5. Association between gray matter morphological abnormalities of the identified subtypes and distribution of neurotransmitter receptors/transporters. (A). Multilinear models were constructed to analyze the association between neurotransmitter receptors/transporters and gray matter morphological abnormalities of the identified subtypes. The bar plot displays the corresponding model goodness-of-fit (adjusted R 2 ), with all FDR-corrected permutation p -values being &lt; 0.001. (B). Dominance analysis was employed to determine the relative importance of predictors for each multilinear model. Total dominance values, indicative of the predictors ' relative importance, are depicted. (C). Receptors were categorized into excitatory and inhibitory types, and their accumulated contributions to gray matter morphological abnormalities of the identified subtypes were computed by summing the total dominance values. The accumulated contribution of excitatory receptors (Excitatory) is plotted against that of inhibitory receptors (Inhibitory), with the gray line representing the identity line.
Fang2024.pdf.md:268:- Gallezot, J.D., Nabulsi, N., Neumeister, A., Planeta-Wilson, B., Williams, W.A., Singhal, T., Kim, S., Maguire, R.P., McCarthy, T., Frost, J.J., Huang, Y., Ding, Y.S., Carson, R.E., 2010. Kinetic modeling of the serotonin 5-HT(1B) receptor radioligand [(11)C]P943 in humans. Journal of cerebral blood flow and metabolism : official journal of the International Society of Cerebral Blood Flow and Metabolism 30, 196 -210.
Fang2024.pdf.md:275:- Han, S., Cui, Q., Zheng, R., Li, S., Zhou, B., Fang, K., Sheng, W., Wen, B., Liu, L., Wei, Y., Chen, H., 2023a. Parsing altered gray matter morphology of depression using a framework integrating the normative model and non-negative matrix factorization. 14, 4053.
Fang2024.pdf.md:303:- Marquand, A.F., Rezek, I., Buitelaar, J., Beckmann, C.F., 2016. Understanding heterogeneity in clinical cohorts using normative models: beyond case-control studies. Biol. Psychiatry 80, 552 -561.
